1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,224
This session follows the school ECG and blood-gas sections of Seven D
Part B. Use the earlier rhythm and lead-placement skills, but now
connect electrical and chemical findings to one changing patient.

2
00:00:13,224 --> 00:00:24,216
The goal is not to collect more labels. Explain whether a result is
reliable, what process it supports and what clinical threat needs
attention while uncertainty remains.

3
00:00:24,216 --> 00:00:36,960
You will classify four basic acid-base patterns and see why a normal
pH or a nondiagnostic ECG does not guarantee safety. The pattern is a
tool, not the complete diagnosis.

4
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:50,400
Interpretation teaching does not grant invasive sampling, analyzer
use, advanced medication or an automatic destination bypass. Keep the
actual role and pathway visible throughout.

5
00:00:50,400 --> 00:01:02,760
Confirm patient identity, acquisition time and the symptom or
treatment state. A trace from before a major clinical change may
remain useful but does not describe the whole current encounter.

6
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:14,856
Check electrode placement, missing leads, movement artifact and
baseline wander. Poor placement can create a convincing pattern that
more confident interpretation will not repair.

7
00:01:14,856 --> 00:01:25,224
Use the actual recording speed and gain when measuring intervals and
elevation. The standard teaching grid is only valid when the printed
calibration agrees with it.

8
00:01:25,224 --> 00:01:37,152
Obtain the useful recording promptly without abandoning airway,
breathing or perfusion care. A fast unreadable printout is not a
completed diagnostic ECG.

9
00:01:37,152 --> 00:01:50,616
Start with rate and regularity, then identify atrial activity and its
relationship to ventricular activation. Do not diagnose an AV block
from one dropped-looking complex without checking the pattern.

10
00:01:50,616 --> 00:02:04,272
Measure PR from the start of P to the start of QRS and assess QRS
width using the calibration. A label generated by the device is a
prompt to review, not an independent final interpretation.

11
00:02:04,272 --> 00:02:16,368
Review ST and T changes by anatomically related leads, not one
isolated spike. The J point marks the end of QRS and the beginning of
the ST segment.

12
00:02:16,368 --> 00:02:27,720
Then state whether the patient is stable or deteriorating and whether
the rhythm is causing, contributing to or compensating for the
problem. Rate alone does not settle that distinction.

13
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:41,160
The school uses ischemia, injury and infarction to connect oxygen
deprivation with possible ECG changes. Learn the biological ideas
without assuming every patient displays a predictable sequence.

14
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:54,696
Hyperacute or inverted T waves and ST depression can be concerning in
context. These findings are not exclusive to one disease, and a
dangerous coronary event can initially have a nondiagnostic tracing.

15
00:02:54,696 --> 00:03:07,656
ST elevation can reflect acute ischemic injury but has mimics;
pathological Q waves may reflect infarction but do not always appear,
remain permanent or prove the event time by themselves.

16
00:03:07,656 --> 00:03:19,848
Do not wait for the next supposed cascade stage before escalating
concern. The useful decision comes from the whole tracing, symptoms,
physiology and the authorized acute-coronary pathway.

17
00:03:19,848 --> 00:03:33,096
The school emphasizes new J-point ST elevation in at least two
contiguous leads. Apply the lead-specific thresholds learned earlier
rather than a single one-millimeter rule for every lead and patient.

18
00:03:33,096 --> 00:03:44,256
Contiguous means anatomically related views, not simply adjacent boxes
on the printed page. Inferior Two, Three and a V F form one familiar
group.

19
00:03:44,256 --> 00:03:57,576
Reciprocal depression can strengthen concern but is not required in
every true event. Also check symptoms, old traces when available and
important mimics or conduction abnormalities.

20
00:03:57,576 --> 00:04:10,632
Use the actual STEMI notification and destination criteria promptly.
Do not wait for a field troponin or promise that every suspected
pattern automatically bypasses every local hospital.

21
00:04:10,632 --> 00:04:24,216
Review the standard territory groups so you can state where the
abnormality is seen. Regional views overlap and coronary anatomy
varies, so one ECG label is not angiographic proof of the culprit
artery.

22
00:04:24,216 --> 00:04:35,544
High lateral changes in One and a V L may meet conventional STEMI
criteria. They should not all be called an equivalent simply because
the school places them in an advanced review.

23
00:04:35,544 --> 00:04:49,008
Posterior or right-sided concern may require additional trained views
and consultation. Keep the standard recording and label additional
positions clearly, while urgent care and notification continue.

24
00:04:49,008 --> 00:05:02,880
The later advanced-twelve-lead workshop develops posterior and
conduction-confounded patterns. Here, state the anatomical concern and
uncertainty without pretending one absent clue excludes disease.

25
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:15,264
A nondiagnostic ECG does not exclude acute coronary syndrome. NSTEMI
can have important ST or T abnormalities, and not every troponin
elevation is an infarction.

26
00:05:15,264 --> 00:05:28,512
Unstable angina and infarction require more context than pain
intensity or one tracing. A painless or unusual symptom description
should not make new hypoperfusion or breathlessness reassuring.

27
00:05:28,512 --> 00:05:42,696
The current acute-coronary guidance explicitly states that new or
presumed new left bundle branch block is not diagnostic of acute
infarction in isolation. Clinical correlation and trained
interpretation remain necessary.

28
00:05:42,696 --> 00:05:55,872
Repeat useful ECGs when symptoms persist or change through the actual
pathway. Early escalation is appropriate when concern remains high,
even while a complete diagnosis is unresolved.

29
00:05:55,872 --> 00:06:07,848
The school lists second-degree type Two block as a dangerous pattern.
A dropped QRS alone is insufficient: identify atrial activity and the
PR relationship in conducted beats.

30
00:06:07,848 --> 00:06:20,352
A two-to-one pattern may not reveal enough conducted sequences to
distinguish the type. Describe the uncertainty and patient state
rather than confidently assigning a subtype from missing evidence.

31
00:06:20,352 --> 00:06:33,888
Complete heart block shows atrial and ventricular activity proceeding
independently in the appropriate tracing. Escape rate and QRS
appearance matter, but the patient perfusion determines the immediate
threat.

32
00:06:33,888 --> 00:06:47,208
Recognize deterioration, support primary needs and seek advanced help
through the actual protocol. Do not import a generic pacing or
atropine instruction into a PCP role without its required authority.

33
00:06:47,208 --> 00:06:58,920
Wide-complex tachycardia can be a dangerous ventricular rhythm, but
assess whether a pulse is present and whether perfusion is failing. A
rate label cannot replace the patient assessment.

34
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:11,976
Rapid atrial fibrillation is usually irregularly irregular with no
consistent sinus P waves, but artifact and other rhythms can mislead.
Look at a useful tracing and the whole clinical state.

35
00:07:11,976 --> 00:07:23,088
A bleeding, hypoxic or infected patient may be tachycardic in
compensation. Treating the number reflexively can worsen an already
inadequate circulation.

36
00:07:23,088 --> 00:07:36,792
Pulseless, unstable monomorphic and unstable polymorphic rhythms do
not all use the same electrical action. Recognize and communicate
accurately while following the trained role-specific pathway.

37
00:07:36,792 --> 00:08:28,992
The literal 17.6 opening groups drugs outside the four main Vaughan
Williams classes, but they do not share one mechanism. Atropine
reduces muscarinic vagal effects and can increase the rate in selected
symptomatic bradycardia. Adenosine briefly slows AV-nodal conduction
in selected tachycardia pathways; its very short action explains the
trained rapid-delivery and flush process, not permission to give it
for every fast or wide rhythm. Digoxin can slow AV conduction and has
positive inotropic effects; renal impairment, electrolyte disturbances
and interactions raise toxicity concerns. The selected manufacturer
mechanism and renal sections support that distinction. None is
interchangeable with a class-III drug, and this reading adds no
independent PCP administration order.

38
00:08:28,992 --> 00:09:12,672
Correct the chapter-summary pressure model before using it in the
weekly case. Cardiac output equals heart rate times stroke volume;
mean pressure above right-atrial pressure is approximately cardiac
output times systemic vascular resistance. The source summary naming
only stroke volume and resistance omits heart rate. In an original
arithmetic example, 100 beats/minute times 50 mL/beat is 5 L/minute. A
lower rate does not guarantee better perfusion if stroke volume cannot
compensate. This is a simplified steady-flow explanation, not an exact
formula for predicting systolic pressure or deciding a fluid or drug
dose.

39
00:09:12,672 --> 00:09:25,872
A single ECG is a sample in time. New pressure, syncope, worsening
breathing or reduced perfusion can justify repeating it through the
indicated pathway while primary care continues.

40
00:09:25,872 --> 00:09:38,280
Compare recordings with attention to placement and quality. A changed
electrode position can look like a physiological change and should not
be silently treated as definite disease evolution.

41
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:49,824
Record actual symptom and treatment times so the receiving team can
interpret the sequence. Preserve both tracings rather than discarding
the earlier one once a new pattern appears.

42
00:09:49,824 --> 00:10:00,528
Communicate the concerning change and current patient state promptly.
Uncertainty can be stated clearly without delaying the notification
until every label is certain.

43
00:10:00,528 --> 00:10:10,032
A blood gas can add evidence about oxygenation, ventilation and acid-
base balance. These are related questions but not the same variable.

44
00:10:10,032 --> 00:10:22,608
PaO2 reflects dissolved arterial oxygen tension, not all tissue oxygen
delivery. A normal value does not correct severe anemia, low cardiac
output or impaired cellular use.

45
00:10:22,608 --> 00:10:34,416
Carbon dioxide helps assess the balance between production and
effective ventilation. Its interpretation must include the sample
type, perfusion and the recent respiratory treatment.

46
00:10:34,416 --> 00:10:47,448
pH and bicarbonate help describe acid-base processes. None of these
numbers replaces a primary assessment of whether the patient is
becoming more confused, exhausted or hypoperfused.

47
00:10:47,448 --> 00:10:57,792
An ABG uses an arterial specimen. Confirm the actual source and
collection time rather than assuming that a blood-gas printout must be
arterial.

48
00:10:57,792 --> 00:11:11,064
Record oxygen device and settings, ventilation support and important
recent changes. A PaO2 obtained on substantial supplemental oxygen
means something different from the same number on room air.

49
00:11:11,064 --> 00:11:22,968
Use the laboratory or analyzer reference and units. A report may
display kilopascals instead of millimeters of mercury, and an apparent
extreme value can be a unit error.

50
00:11:22,968 --> 00:11:34,848
Knowing the interpretation is distinct from being authorized and
competent to collect an arterial specimen or operate that analyzer. Do
not attempt invasive collection based only on this lesson.

51
00:11:34,848 --> 00:11:46,896
A VBG uses a venous specimen and can support selected acid-base
questions. The school correctly warns that arterial and venous results
are not interchangeable in every respect.

52
00:11:46,896 --> 00:12:00,576
Venous carbon dioxide and pH are not guaranteed to equal the arterial
values. Sampling site and poor perfusion can widen differences, so do
not use a universal add-or-subtract correction.

53
00:12:00,576 --> 00:12:13,416
Venous PO2 does not describe arterial oxygenation. Do not apply an
arterial eighty-to-one-hundred oxygen range to the venous result and
declare respiratory failure from that comparison.

54
00:12:13,416 --> 00:12:25,896
State exactly what the sample supports and what remains unknown. Use
the patient, reliable oximetry and an appropriate arterial measurement
when that is actually required and available.

55
00:12:25,896 --> 00:12:37,248
Check identity, source and time before interpreting a surprising gas.
A mislabeled venous specimen cannot be made arterial by applying the
expected range.

56
00:12:37,248 --> 00:12:51,024
Air contamination, inadequate sample, anticoagulant problems and
delayed analysis can change results. Use the actual collection and
analyzer instructions rather than a generic storage rule.

57
00:12:51,024 --> 00:13:03,456
Compare with the clinical state and other reliable observations, and
seek an appropriate repeat or laboratory review when needed. Do not
repeatedly sample in a way that delays necessary support.

58
00:13:03,456 --> 00:13:15,528
Record the uncertainty so the next team does not treat a questionable
number as a confirmed fact. Validity is part of interpretation, not a
separate optional administrative task.

59
00:13:15,528 --> 00:13:27,120
The saved school section uses pH seven point three five to seven point
four five, arterial carbon dioxide thirty-five to forty-five, and
bicarbonate twenty-two to twenty-six.

60
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:41,232
Its arterial oxygen example is eighty to one hundred millimeters of
mercury. Age, altitude, inspired oxygen and the clinical condition
affect the expected oxygen result; this is not a venous reference.

61
00:13:41,232 --> 00:13:55,488
The candidate A and P table has different bicarbonate and carbon-
dioxide ranges. Keep the school teaching range and the actual
laboratory reference labelled instead of blending them into a new
invented range.

62
00:13:55,488 --> 00:14:08,448
A value outside a reference range is not a complete diagnosis; a value
inside it does not exclude serious illness. The next step is clinical
interpretation and the pattern across the result.

63
00:14:08,448 --> 00:14:18,792
PaO2 is not a direct tissue-oxygen measurement, despite one school
flashcard wording. It describes oxygen tension in the sampled arterial
blood.

64
00:14:18,792 --> 00:14:32,352
Saturation describes the proportion of hemoglobin binding sites
carrying oxygen. Content also depends on how much hemoglobin is
present, so severe anemia can reduce carriage despite a good
saturation.

65
00:14:32,352 --> 00:14:43,248
Delivery also requires effective circulation. A patient in shock may
have an acceptable oxygen tension while tissues still receive too
little oxygenated blood.

66
00:14:43,248 --> 00:14:52,704
This distinction matters in the school cardiac-trauma integration.
Correcting an oxygen number does not control hemorrhage or restore a
failing pump.

67
00:14:52,704 --> 00:15:34,584
At the assigned 22.6 boundary, distinguish hyperpnea from
hyperventilation. Hyperpnea is increased breathing appropriate to
increased metabolic demand; exercise can increase ventilation through
anticipatory neural, motor and proprioceptive inputs before a large
fall in oxygen occurs. Hyperventilation means effective alveolar
ventilation exceeds carbon dioxide production and tends to lower
PaCO2. Rate alone cannot distinguish them. The source sentence saying
exercise does not cause hyperpnea conflicts with its own discussion;
learn the demand-versus-ventilation relationship rather than that
absolute sentence.

68
00:15:34,584 --> 00:16:19,824
The same page introduces high altitude: the oxygen fraction of air
remains approximately 21 percent while lower barometric pressure
lowers inspired oxygen partial pressure. That reduces the available
oxygen gradient; it does not mean the atmosphere changed to a smaller
oxygen percentage. In the weekly diagnostic case, record altitude,
oxygen delivery and exertion with the saturation or blood gas. A
textbook example of saturation at one altitude is not an acceptable
target for an acutely ill patient. The following altitude table and
full acclimatization discussion lie beyond this literal page ending
and are not silently added as another completed assigned range.

69
00:16:19,824 --> 00:17:08,112
Read the ending glossary as connected physiology. ICF is intracellular
fluid; IF is interstitial fluid, and ECF includes IF and plasma.
Dehydration means insufficient body water, whereas diuresis means
increased urine output; neither term alone specifies sodium
concentration or emergency fluid needs. ADH, also called vasopressin,
supports renal water conservation, and active vitamin D supports
intestinal calcium absorption. The source's generic fluid-compartment
definition describes the intracellular example too narrowly: plasma
and interstitial fluid are also compartments. Match each space,
membrane and driving force before predicting where water will move.

70
00:17:08,112 --> 00:17:54,384
Decode the paired laboratory terms rather than memorizing a symptom as
a diagnosis. Hyper- means above and hypo- below the applicable blood
reference: natremia for sodium, kalemia for potassium, chloremia for
chloride, calcemia for calcium and phosphatemia for phosphate.
Hypercapnia and hypocapnia concern carbon dioxide, not oxygen
saturation. Respiratory and metabolic acid-base terms identify a
process; measured acidemia or alkalemia is a separate statement. In
the original breathless patient, low SpO2 does not establish
hypercapnia, and confusion does not prove hyponatremia; use the actual
measurement and clinical context.

71
00:17:54,384 --> 00:18:07,896
Carbon dioxide and water are linked through carbonic acid to hydrogen
ions and bicarbonate. Carbonic anhydrase speeds the reversible
reaction; this connects gas transport and buffering.

72
00:18:07,896 --> 00:18:21,528
At otherwise comparable conditions, retaining carbon dioxide tends to
increase acidity, while greater effective removal tends to raise pH.
Do not copy the candidate figure caption that reverses the latter
direction.

73
00:18:21,528 --> 00:18:33,672
Bicarbonate buffers added acid, and the kidneys reclaim and generate
bicarbonate while excreting acid. This is not a reason to give
bicarbonate automatically whenever the pH is low.

74
00:18:33,672 --> 00:18:44,784
Chemical buffers respond rapidly, breathing adjustments over minutes
and renal changes over longer periods. Time and organ function
determine the observed pattern.

75
00:18:44,784 --> 00:18:58,152
Minute ventilation is rate times tidal volume, but not all that volume
reaches gas-exchanging alveoli. Dead-space ventilation explains why
rapid shallow breathing can still clear carbon dioxide poorly.

76
00:18:58,152 --> 00:19:10,344
In obstruction, slow exhalation and air trapping can limit effective
ventilation. A patient can have a high respiratory rate and still
develop serious carbon dioxide retention.

77
00:19:10,344 --> 00:19:22,824
Watch effort, tidal volume, air entry and awareness together. A
falling rate with worsening fatigue is not necessarily the return to a
healthy normal breathing pattern.

78
00:19:22,824 --> 00:19:32,880
Support ineffective breathing promptly through the trained pathway.
Supplemental oxygen may improve saturation without solving the
ventilation failure.

79
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:43,104
First decide whether the measured pH is below, above or within the
chosen reference. Acidemia and alkalemia describe that observed state.

80
00:19:43,104 --> 00:19:56,856
Acidosis and alkalosis describe processes that tend to lower or raise
pH. Several processes may occur together, so a patient can have an
acidosis even when the final pH is not below the range.

81
00:19:56,856 --> 00:20:07,560
A low pH does not by itself identify a metabolic cause. Look at carbon
dioxide, bicarbonate, the clinical history and the possibility of a
mixed disorder.

82
00:20:07,560 --> 00:20:19,680
Avoid the candidate fixed fatal-pH statements as universal boundaries.
A severe derangement warrants urgent patient-linked assessment, not a
deterministic prediction from one number.

83
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:29,016
Respiratory Opposite means that primary carbon dioxide retention tends
to lower pH, while primary carbon dioxide loss tends to raise it.

84
00:20:29,016 --> 00:20:40,392
Metabolic Equal compares pH with primary bicarbonate change: lower
bicarbonate tends to accompany lower pH, and higher bicarbonate tends
to accompany higher pH.

85
00:20:40,392 --> 00:20:50,952
ROME does not mean all three numbers must move together or that a
compensatory change is a second primary diagnosis. Use the whole
pattern and the clinical story.

86
00:20:50,952 --> 00:21:01,872
It also does not judge arterial oxygenation or exclude mixed
disorders. Finish by explaining what the patient needs, not by
announcing that the mnemonic has been completed.

87
00:21:01,872 --> 00:21:14,928
A basic respiratory acidosis pattern is low pH with high carbon
dioxide. It can occur with respiratory depression, severe obstruction
or another failure of effective alveolar ventilation.

88
00:21:14,928 --> 00:21:26,496
Early bicarbonate may be near baseline; with chronic adaptation it may
rise. A high bicarbonate does not automatically turn the result into
primary metabolic alkalosis.

89
00:21:26,496 --> 00:21:38,832
Look for shallow breathing, fatigue, reduced air entry and falling
awareness. A patient receiving oxygen may keep a reassuring saturation
while carbon dioxide rises.

90
00:21:38,832 --> 00:21:50,904
Provide the indicated trained ventilation support and cause-
appropriate care; do not wait for another sample before treating
obvious failure. Reassess response and arrange additional help.

91
00:21:50,904 --> 00:22:02,424
The simple pattern is high pH and low carbon dioxide from ventilation
exceeding carbon dioxide production. Lightheadedness or tingling can
occur but does not identify the cause.

92
00:22:02,424 --> 00:22:15,360
Consider hypoxemia, infection, pulmonary vascular disease, pain or
toxins as well as anxiety. A frightened patient can still have a
serious medical cause of rapid breathing.

93
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:27,504
Do not use paper-bag rebreathing to correct the number. It can reduce
inspired oxygen and harm patients whose apparent hyperventilation
reflects hypoxemia or myocardial ischemia.

94
00:22:27,504 --> 00:22:39,480
Reassure while assessing rather than using reassurance as a diagnostic
test. The destination and urgency depend on the complete evaluation,
not a presumed benign label.

95
00:22:39,480 --> 00:22:52,944
A basic metabolic acidosis pattern has low pH with low bicarbonate.
Added acid, gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss or impaired renal acid
excretion can produce this relationship.

96
00:22:52,944 --> 00:23:03,384
Consider the mechanism in the history: dehydration with ketones,
shock-related illness, diarrhea or renal failure are not all the same
disease or the same fluid plan.

97
00:23:03,384 --> 00:23:16,152
The respiratory response may lower carbon dioxide through increased
effective ventilation. Deep breathing may be compensatory, not merely
emotional distress that should be slowed without assessment.

98
00:23:16,152 --> 00:23:29,064
Treat immediate threats and the cause through the authorized pathway.
A low bicarbonate is not an automatic order for bicarbonate
medication, and a normalizing pH is not proof the cause is gone.

99
00:23:29,064 --> 00:23:41,472
A basic metabolic alkalosis pattern has high pH with relatively high
bicarbonate. Gastric acid loss, certain diuretics and other renal or
hormonal processes can contribute.

100
00:23:41,472 --> 00:23:52,728
Ask about vomiting, medications, intake and the underlying illness. Do
not assume vomiting and diarrhea create the same acid-base pattern
because both lose fluid.

101
00:23:52,728 --> 00:24:04,656
Respiratory compensation may retain carbon dioxide, but adequate
oxygenation limits that response. Deliberately creating inadequate
breathing is not a routine corrective maneuver.

102
00:24:04,656 --> 00:24:15,936
Use the patient state, electrolytes when available and the actual care
pathway. A single direction label does not supply a safe replacement-
fluid or medication prescription.

103
00:24:15,936 --> 00:24:29,760
The body can move pH toward baseline through another system. In
primary respiratory disease, renal bicarbonate handling adapts over
time; in primary metabolic disease, ventilation can respond sooner.

104
00:24:29,760 --> 00:24:40,032
Time and function matter. Chronic carbon dioxide retention and a
sudden new ventilatory failure do not have the same expected
bicarbonate pattern.

105
00:24:40,032 --> 00:24:52,512
A compensatory direction does not prove the response is appropriate in
size, and a normal pH does not prove a benign fully compensated state.
More than one primary problem may coexist.

106
00:24:52,512 --> 00:25:05,352
This introductory lesson does not replace a full mixed-disorder
assessment. Explain the likely pattern and uncertainty, then use the
appropriate clinical review instead of overstating certainty.

107
00:25:05,352 --> 00:25:19,416
In an original assumed arterial result, pH is seven point four zero,
carbon dioxide twenty and bicarbonate twelve. The pH is within range,
but two major variables are clearly abnormal.

108
00:25:19,416 --> 00:25:30,864
Do not stop at normal or automatically call the patient fully
compensated. Opposing respiratory and metabolic processes can produce
a deceptively ordinary final pH.

109
00:25:30,864 --> 00:25:44,040
A trained expected-compensation review can reveal that the carbon
dioxide is lower than expected for a simple metabolic acidosis. The
likely interpretation needs the clinical story, not the mnemonic
alone.

110
00:25:44,040 --> 00:25:54,024
The case teaches why you preserve the full report and seek appropriate
help. It does not turn a classroom example into a medication or
destination rule.

111
00:25:54,024 --> 00:26:06,888
Take another original assumed arterial result: pH seven point two
eight, carbon dioxide thirty-one and bicarbonate fourteen. First
identify acidemia and the low bicarbonate.

112
00:26:06,888 --> 00:26:17,280
The pattern supports a metabolic acidosis with a respiratory response,
rather than calling the low carbon dioxide a separate respiratory
alkalosis automatically.

113
00:26:17,280 --> 00:26:28,992
The linked reading shows a limited expected-response calculation for
this example. In practice, the cause and the patient state still
determine the urgency and the authorized treatment.

114
00:26:28,992 --> 00:26:41,448
Look for the history that fits, such as losses, renal illness or
metabolic disease, while supporting perfusion and watching breathing.
Do not invent a diagnosis from these three numbers alone.

115
00:26:41,448 --> 00:26:55,344
In an original community-theatre encounter, a sixty-four-year-old with
obstructive lung disease becomes exhausted. Supplemental oxygen
improves saturation, but breaths become smaller and awareness falls.

116
00:26:55,344 --> 00:27:10,368
For the classroom, assume an arterial result of pH seven point two
five, carbon dioxide sixty and bicarbonate about twenty-six. This
supports a predominantly respiratory acidosis with inadequate
ventilation.

117
00:27:10,368 --> 00:27:23,352
The falling respiratory rate is not reassuring in this trajectory.
Reassess airway, breathing and circulation, provide effective trained
ventilation support and seek appropriate advanced assistance.

118
00:27:23,352 --> 00:27:33,936
Do not delay that response to obtain a gas in an unstable patient; the
example explains a process that clinical deterioration already
requires you to recognize.

119
00:27:33,936 --> 00:27:45,768
The Week Six Wednesday work includes a rapid ETCO2 fall after return
of circulation. Treat that as a prompt for immediate reassessment, not
one certain diagnosis.

120
00:27:45,768 --> 00:28:00,312
Assess the patient for recurrent arrest or reduced cardiac output
while checking airway, ventilation and the sampling circuit. A
disconnection, altered ventilation or poor perfusion can produce
different responses.

121
00:28:00,312 --> 00:28:13,056
Do not increase ventilation reflexively because the carbon dioxide is
already low. Overventilation can worsen a circulation problem; respond
to the actual cause and clinical state.

122
00:28:13,056 --> 00:28:25,608
Keep school post-ROSC targets distinct from current local guideline
differences already taught in Week Five. A target band never replaces
immediate recognition of an ineffective breath or pulse.

123
00:28:25,608 --> 00:28:39,312
The school uses pH below seven point two zero as a red-flag example.
Marked derangement deserves urgent attention, but the number alone is
not a universal ICU-destination or impending-arrest rule.

124
00:28:39,312 --> 00:28:51,744
Determine the patient needs now and the capability needed next.
Geography, instability, available support and actual regional
agreements affect the safest receiving pathway.

125
00:28:51,744 --> 00:29:03,912
Mild respiratory alkalosis does not prove anxiety or a safe routine
destination, and apparently compensated COPD does not establish safety
during a new deterioration.

126
00:29:03,912 --> 00:29:14,232
Do not delay needed support or conveyance while waiting for an
unavailable test. Give an early, patient-linked pre-alert and
coordinate the appropriate resource.

127
00:29:14,232 --> 00:29:26,328
Give the receiving team a coherent sequence, not an ECG label followed
by disconnected laboratory numbers. Start with the patient and why the
situation is time-sensitive.

128
00:29:26,328 --> 00:29:39,216
Describe the interpretable tracing and what changed, then give the
actual gas type, values, units, time and oxygen or ventilation state.
State any sample-quality concern explicitly.

129
00:29:39,216 --> 00:29:52,128
Distinguish observation from interpretation, including the likely
acid-base process and unresolved alternatives. Avoid reporting venous
oxygen as arterial or a normal pH as no disorder.

130
00:29:52,128 --> 00:30:04,872
Finish with actual treatment, response and the next request, and
confirm the message was received. A precise handover supports
continued care while definitive diagnosis is still developing.

131
00:30:04,872 --> 00:30:17,784
You should be able to read a useful ECG systematically, recognize
important ischemic or rhythm concerns and explain why neither a
guaranteed cascade nor a single normal tracing is safe reasoning.

132
00:30:17,784 --> 00:30:28,536
For a gas, identify sample type and context before using reference
values. Separate oxygen tension from saturation, content and delivery.

133
00:30:28,536 --> 00:30:42,864
Explain the four primary patterns and why compensation or normal pH
needs further context. Use the original worked examples and bilingual
reading to reconstruct the mechanism rather than memorizing arrows
alone.

134
00:30:42,864 --> 00:30:56,448
Finally name the actual threat and next action, including when to stop
testing and support the patient. The remainder of the weekend applies
this reasoning to advanced respiratory, twelve-lead and multisystem
practice.
